          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I am 68 with COPD and I have been more breathless for three days with green sputum. Is this an emergency, and what does guidance describe about exacerbations?",
          "answer": "**Please contact your doctor, COPD nurse, or local urgent service today.** The general COPD reference says that worsening breathlessness that does not respond to your usual reliever inhaler needs urgent assessment rather than a routine appointment. An automated check of your description returned \"routine,\" but that is not a clearance. If you have a written COPD action plan, follow it.\n\n**Call emergency services now (999, 911, or your local number) if you have any of these:**\n- Breathlessness at rest, or difficulty speaking in full sentences\n- Drowsiness, confusion, or unusual sleepiness, especially while struggling to breathe. The reference notes this can signal a rise in carbon dioxide, not just tiredness.\n- Blue or grey lips or fingers\n- Chest pain, or coughing up blood\n- Fever with a marked change in your phlegm\n\nWhile you wait for help, sit upright, loosen tight clothing, keep calm, and use your reliever as your action plan says. These points come from the COPD reference and the red-flag guidance on severe breathing difficulty.\n\n**What the guidance says about exacerbations:** The general reference describes an exacerbation as an acute worsening of respiratory symptoms beyond your normal day-to-day variation. It is usually triggered by a viral or bacterial infection, can worsen quality of life and speed up lung decline, and can take weeks to recover from. The COPD guideline says a past exacerbation is the best predictor of a future one, and that your history of exacerbations, especially any that needed hospital admission, helps decide your treatment group.\n\n**What the retrieved material does not cover:** I found no definition of an exacerbation that uses sputum colour or volume, so I cannot say what the guidance says about green sputum alone. I also found no guidance on when antibiotics or steroids are advised, so please do not assume either is needed; ask your clinician. The passages do not state how strong the evidence behind these recommendations is. I found no disagreement between authorities on COPD exacerbation definitions or treatment.\n\n**Questions to ask your clinician:** Do you have a fever? Has your sputum changed in amount or thickness, not just colour? Is your reliever inhaler working as well as usual? Your answers will help them decide how urgently you need to be seen.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
